To draw a labeled diagram of an axon terminal and synapse for this question:

- **Presynaptic Membrane**: The outermost layer of the cell body containing the nucleus.
- **Synaptic Cleft**: A narrow space between the presynaptic and postsynaptic membranes.
- **Post-Synaptic Membrane**: The inner surface of the postsynaptic neuron's dendrites or soma.
- **Receptors**: Specialized proteins on the post-synaptic membrane capable of binding specific neurotransmitters.
- **Neurotransmitters**: Chemical messengers released by the presynaptic neuron.

Here’s a simple representation:
$$\begin{array}{c}
\textbf{Axon Terminal} \\
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=2cm]
    \draw[fill] (-1,-1) circle [radius=.5];
    \node[left] {Presynaptic Membrane};

    \draw[thick,dashed] (-1,-1) -- ++(1,0);
    \node[right] {\textbf{Synaptic Cleft}};

    \draw[fill] (1,-1) circle [radius=.5];
    \node[right] {Postsynaptic Membrane};

    \draw[dotted] (-1,-1) -- (1,-1); 

    \foreach \x/\y in {(-1/-1/Receptor)}
        \node[circle,inner sep=1pt,label={[label distance=-1mm]\x:\y}] (\x) {};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{array}$$

In this diagram, the left side represents the presynaptic neuron, while the right side is part of the postsynaptic neuron. Neurotransmitters are represented as small dots moving from the presynaptic to the postsynaptic side across the synaptic cleft. Receptors are indicated at the postsynaptic membrane. This depiction shows how impulses can be transmitted from one neuron to another through these structures.

